    "cons": [
      "No electric brake, so the wheel coasts after you switch off",
      "Lock-on slide switch rather than a no-lock paddle"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A well-proven cordless grinder for Makita owners, as long as you are comfortable with a slide switch and no brake.",
    "description": "The XAG04Z is one of the most popular cordless grinders on Amazon, with around 3,100 ratings averaging 4.7, and it has earned that reputation by being consistently strong on Makita's 18V LXT batteries. Its brushless motor runs at 8,500 RPM, it accepts 4-1/2-inch and 5-inch wheels on a 5/8-11 spindle and Makita's automatic speed change technology raises torque and lowers speed under load so the motor keeps working rather than bogging down. Retail listings put it at about 14-1/4 inches long and roughly 5.6 lbs with a battery. The reason it is not our top pick is its safety package. It uses a slide switch with a lock-on function rather than a no-lock paddle, so if you drop it while locked on, it keeps running. It also has no electric brake, so the wheel coasts to a stop after you switch off, which is a hazard if you set the grinder down too soon. Many experienced users prefer a slide switch for long grinding sessions, and the XAG04Z is a fine tool in their hands. For an occasional user, we prefer a paddle switch with a brake. Makita sells other LXT grinders with paddle switches and brakes if you want them on the same batteries."
  },
  {
    "id": "dewalt-dwe402",
    "name": "DeWalt 4-1/2\" 11 Amp Paddle Switch Small Angle Grinder (DWE402)",
    "category": "4-1/2\", 11 Amp Corded, Paddle",
    "badge": "Best Corded",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 5400,
    "pros": [
      "11 amp, 11,000 RPM motor never runs out of battery",
      "Paddle switch with a safety lock-off to prevent accidental starts",
      "One-touch guard rotates 360 degrees without tools",
      "Quick-change wheel release removes wheels without a wrench",
      "About 5,400 Amazon ratings averaging 4.7"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No brake or electronic kickback protection",
      "The cord limits where you can work and can be cut by the wheel"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A powerful, inexpensive corded grinder for a garage or shop with outlets close at hand.",
    "description": "If your grinder will mostly be used in a garage, a corded tool like the DWE402 gives you more power for less money than any cordless option. It has an 11 amp motor spinning a 4-1/2-inch wheel at 11,000 RPM, with 1,400 max watts out according to DeWalt, which is more than enough for cutting rebar, removing rust with a wire wheel or flap disc and grinding welds. The paddle switch has a safety lock-off, so the tool will not start accidentally when you pick it up. Two convenience features make it easier to use safely: a one-touch guard that rotates 360 degrees without tools, so you are more likely to position it correctly, and a quick-change wheel release that lets you remove wheels without a wrench. A dust ejection system clears debris from the motor intake, which extends tool life in dirty work. It is one of the most popular grinders on Amazon, with around 5,400 ratings averaging 4.7. What it lacks is the electronic safety of the newer cordless tools: there is no brake and no kickback sensing. Use the side handle, keep the guard between you and the wheel and keep the cord behind you, away from the cut."
