    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 4900,
    "pros": [
      "About 4 lbs, light and easy to control",
      "10,000 RPM, 980 W motor",
      "Kit includes five grinding wheels and a case",
      "Five-year warranty from Metabo HPT",
      "Spindle lock for easy wheel changes"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Only 6.2 amps, so it slows under heavy grinding",
      "Slide switch rather than a no-lock paddle, and no brake"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "An inexpensive, light grinder for occasional rust removal, bolt cutting and blade sharpening.",
    "description": "If you need a grinder a few times a year and do not own a battery platform, the Metabo HPT G12SR4 is the cheapest grinder we would recommend. It is a 6.2 amp, 980-watt grinder spinning a 4-1/2-inch wheel at 10,000 RPM, and at about 4 lbs it is easy to control with one hand on the body and the other on the side handle. The kit includes five grinding wheels and a hard case, and Metabo HPT backs it with a five-year warranty, which is unusually long at this price. A spindle lock makes wheel changes straightforward. For cutting a rusted bolt, cleaning a weld, sharpening a mower blade or removing surface rust with a flap disc, it has enough power. For heavy grinding or cutting thick steel, it will slow down, and an 11 amp corded grinder like the DeWalt DWE402 is the better tool. The main drawback is the switch. It uses a slide switch rather than a no-lock paddle and has no brake, so you must switch it off and let the wheel stop before setting it down. It is highly rated across retailers, but we could not confirm a current Amazon rating count, so the figure shown is conservative."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Angle Grinder",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-angle-grinder/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size angle grinder should I buy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Buy a 4-1/2-inch grinder, or one that accepts both 4-1/2-inch and 5-inch wheels. That size handles cutting bolts, rust removal, sharpening and tile, and it is light enough to control easily. Large 7-inch and 9-inch grinders are for heavy fabrication and concrete work, and most homeowners never need one, or the extra weight that comes with it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a paddle switch better than a slide switch?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, yes. A no-lock paddle switch stops the grinder when you let go, so a dropped grinder shuts off. A slide switch with lock-on keeps running until switched off. Experienced users sometimes prefer slide switches for long sessions, but occasional users are safer with a paddle, ideally combined with an electronic brake."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are cordless angle grinders powerful enough?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The best brushless cordless grinders, like the Milwaukee 2880-20 and DeWalt DCG413B, are powerful enough for most work when paired with a large battery. Corded grinders still cost less and run indefinitely, so they suit long sessions at a bench. For occasional jobs around the house and yard, cordless is more convenient and powerful enough."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does a kickback brake do?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A kickback brake senses when the wheel suddenly binds, such as when a cut pinches it, and applies the electronic brake to stop the wheel quickly and shut the tool off. That limits how far the grinder twists in your hands. DeWalt says its DCG413 brake stops the wheel in two seconds or less on average."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use a 5-inch wheel on a 4-1/2-inch grinder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only if the manufacturer says the grinder accepts 5-inch wheels and you fit the matching guard. Some grinders, like the Milwaukee 2880 and Makita XAG04Z, are rated for both. Never remove a guard to fit a larger wheel, and always check that the wheel's maximum RPM is at least the grinder's no-load speed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can an angle grinder cut wood?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can, but we do not recommend it. Woodcarving discs and chain wheels for grinders are among the most dangerous accessories sold, because they catch and kick violently. For wood, use a jigsaw, an oscillating multi-tool or a saw. Keep the grinder for metal, tile, stone and concrete, which is what it is designed for."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I sharpen mower blades with an angle grinder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Clamp the blade firmly in a vise, follow the original bevel with a flap disc or grinding wheel, and avoid overheating the edge; if the steel turns blue, you are pressing too hard. Grind both ends evenly and check the balance afterward. An unbalanced blade vibrates and can damage the mower, so remove metal carefully and evenly."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best angle grinder",
      "cordless angle grinder",
      "paddle switch angle grinder",
      "4-1/2 inch angle grinder",
      "angle grinder safety",
      "corded angle grinder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            7 Grinders Compared
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Angle Grinders</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          An angle grinder cuts bolts, cleans rust, sharpens blades and grinds welds. It is also the most dangerous handheld tool most people own, so the safety features matter as much as the power.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#milwaukee-2880-20"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
