        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#sizes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. 4-1/2 vs 5 vs 7 Inch</a>
          <a href="#switches" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Switches and Brakes</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Our 7 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#choose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. How We Would Choose</a>
          <a href="#discs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Wheels and RPM Ratings</a>
          <a href="#cordless" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Cordless vs Corded</a>
          <a href="#uses" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. What It Is For</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Safety Gear</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most homeowners need an angle grinder a few times a year, for a seized bolt, a rusty trailer or a dull mower blade. For that work, the features that matter most are not power or speed but a paddle switch that stops the tool when you let go and a brake that stops the wheel quickly. This guide ranks grinders with that in mind.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most people, the best angle grinder is a 4-1/2-inch or 5-inch model with a no-lock paddle switch and an electronic brake. The Milwaukee 2880-20 and DeWalt DCG413B both stop the wheel quickly and shut off if you let go, which matters more than raw power for anyone who does not grind every day. Buy whichever matches the batteries you already own.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your grinder will stay in a garage near an outlet, a corded grinder gives you more power for less money. The DeWalt DWE402 is an 11 amp, 11,000 RPM grinder with a paddle switch and a tool-free guard, and it is one of the best-selling grinders on Amazon. Budget buyers should look at the Metabo HPT G12SR4, which is light and comes with a five-year warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            Variable speed is worth paying for if you polish, sand or strip paint as well as cut and grind. A variable-speed model lets you turn the speed down for that work. For cutting bolts, sharpening blades and grinding welds, single-speed grinders are fine.
          </p>

          <p>
            An angle grinder is the most dangerous handheld power tool most homeowners own. It spins an abrasive wheel at around 10,000 RPM with no blade guard on the cutting side, and wheels can shatter. If your job can be done with a hacksaw, a <a href="/best-reciprocating-saw/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">reciprocating saw</a> or an <a href="/best-oscillating-tool/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">oscillating tool</a>, consider that first. If it really needs a grinder, buy one with the safety features below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. 4-1/2 vs 5 vs 7 Inch: Which Size You Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            The number is the maximum wheel diameter. A 4-1/2-inch grinder is the standard size for homeowners and most trades: light, easy to control and compatible with the widest range of wheels and discs. A 5-inch grinder uses slightly larger wheels, which gives a bit more cutting depth and a longer-lasting wheel, at the cost of a little more weight. Many grinders, including the Milwaukee 2880 and Makita XAG04Z, accept both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Large grinders, 7 and 9 inches, are a different class of tool. They are heavy, two-handed tools with large motors, used for heavy fabrication, grinding concrete and cutting thick material. They spin at lower RPM because their bigger wheels travel faster at the rim. They are not good first grinders, and most homeowners will never need one.
          </p>

