        <div id="switches" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Paddle vs Slide Switches, Brakes and Anti-Restart
          </h2>

          <p>
            The switch is the most important safety decision you will make. A no-lock paddle switch runs only while you squeeze it. Let go, and the motor stops. A slide switch with lock-on keeps the grinder running until you push the switch back, so if the grinder kicks out of your hands, it keeps spinning on the floor. Many workplace safety programs require paddle switches for this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Slide switches have defenders, mostly experienced users doing long grinding sessions, because holding a paddle for an hour is tiring. For an occasional user, the paddle is the safer choice. If you buy a slide-switch grinder like the Makita XAG04Z or Metabo HPT G12SR4, never lock it on and always switch it off before setting it down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electronic brakes stop the wheel within a couple of seconds after you release the switch. Without one, a wheel can coast for several seconds, and putting a grinder down while the wheel is still spinning is a common way to damage a bench or catch a leg. Milwaukee's RAPIDSTOP and DeWalt's kickback brake both stop the wheel in about two seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kickback protection and anti-restart features are the other two features worth looking for. Kickback sensing, like the electronic clutch on the Milwaukee, cuts power when the wheel binds and the grinder jerks. Anti-restart, or no-volt release, keeps a grinder from starting by itself when the power returns or a battery is inserted while the switch is on.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">For an occasional user, buy a paddle switch with a brake.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The extra cost is small compared with the cost of an injury, and those two features handle the most common ways grinders hurt people: being dropped while running and being set down too soon.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

