          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How We Would Choose Between Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with batteries. If you already own Milwaukee M18 or DeWalt 20V tools, buy the 2880-20 or the DCG413B and use the packs you have. Makita owners will find the XAG04Z a strong tool, but should consider one of Makita's paddle-switch models if safety features matter to them. Ryobi ONE+ owners get a capable grinder for little money in the PBLAG01B.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you own no battery platform, a corded grinder is the better value. The DeWalt DWE402 is the one we would buy for most people: an 11 amp motor, a paddle switch with lock-off and a tool-free guard. The Metabo HPT G12SR4 costs less and is lighter, at the cost of power and a slide switch. A variable-speed corded model is worth the extra if you polish, sand or strip paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then weigh how often you will grind. An occasional user, someone who cuts a few bolts and sharpens mower blades each year, benefits most from a brake and a no-lock paddle, because unfamiliarity is when accidents happen. A frequent user who grinds for hours may prefer a slide switch for comfort, and should invest in good technique and protective gear to match.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, consider where you will use it. In a garage with outlets, corded is simpler. On a fence line, a trailer or a vehicle in the driveway, cordless saves dragging an extension cord across the work, where the wheel can catch it. For metalwork far from power, a cordless grinder and a couple of large batteries are hard to beat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="discs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Wheels, Discs and RPM Ratings
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every abrasive wheel and disc is marked with a maximum operating speed. That number must be equal to or higher than the no-load RPM of your grinder. A wheel spun faster than its rating can shatter, and fragments leave the wheel at very high speed. Check the rating every time you fit a wheel, particularly if you are using a wheel from a different grinder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the right type of wheel for the job. Thin, flat cut-off wheels are for cutting and must never be used for side grinding, because they are not designed to take side loads and can break. Thicker depressed-center grinding wheels are for grinding. Flap discs are for blending welds and removing rust and paint. Wire wheels and cups strip rust and scale quickly.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
