          <p>
            Cut-off wheels need the correct guard. Many manufacturers specify a closed, two-sided cut-off guard for cutting, and some grinders ship with both a grinding guard and a cut-off guard, as the Ryobi PBLAG01B does. If your grinder came with only one guard, check the manual before cutting with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never fit a saw blade or a woodcarving chain disc to a grinder that was not designed for it. Woodworking discs made for angle grinders exist, but they are among the most dangerous accessories sold and cause severe injuries when they catch. For wood, use a <a href="/best-oscillating-tool/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">multi-tool</a>, a jigsaw or a saw.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cordless" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cordless vs Corded Grinders
          </h2>

          <p>
            Brushless cordless grinders have reached the point where they replace corded tools for most users. A large battery on the Milwaukee or DeWalt delivers similar power to a mid-size corded grinder, and cordless grinders are easier to take to a fence, a trailer or a car in the driveway. They are also safer in one specific way: there is no cord for the wheel to cut.
          </p>

          <p>
            Corded grinders still win on cost and endurance. A good corded grinder costs much less than a cordless tool and battery, and it never runs down in the middle of a long grinding job. If you grind for long periods at a bench, or you do not own a battery platform, a corded grinder is the better buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grinders are among the most power-hungry cordless tools, so battery size matters. Small packs make even the best grinder slow under load. Use a high-capacity pack and expect to swap batteries on long jobs. If you are choosing a battery platform, our <a href="/best-cordless-tool-brand/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best cordless tool brand guide</a> compares the major systems.
          </p>

          <p>
            For automotive work, grinders are useful for cutting off seized exhaust hangers and rusted bolts, but they are not always the best tool. A powerful <a href="/best-impact-wrench/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">impact wrench</a> will often remove a seized nut without destroying it, which saves you cutting and replacing the hardware.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="uses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Homeowners Actually Use a Grinder For
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common household jobs are cutting a seized bolt or padlock, cutting rebar or threaded rod to length, removing rust from trailers, railings and garden tools, sharpening lawn mower blades and cutting tile. A 4-1/2-inch grinder with a handful of cut-off wheels, a flap disc and a wire cup will cover all of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sharpening a mower blade is one of the best reasons to own a grinder. Clamp the blade firmly, follow the original bevel with a flap disc or grinding wheel and check the balance afterward. Our guide on <a href="/how-often-to-sharpen-mower-blades/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">how often to sharpen mower blades</a> covers how often to do it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Always clamp the work. Holding a piece of metal in one hand and a grinder in the other is how wheels catch and grinders kick. A solid <a href="/best-bench-vise/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">bench vise</a> is the safest way to hold small parts for cutting and grinding, and C-clamps work for larger pieces on a bench.
          </p>

          <p>
            For rust removal, start with a wire wheel or cup to remove heavy scale, then a flap disc to smooth the surface. Afterwards, prime bare steel quickly, since it starts to rust again almost immediately. For hand tools, our guide on <a href="/how-to-prevent-rust-on-hand-tools/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">preventing rust on hand tools</a> covers what to do after cleaning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chainsaw chains can be sharpened with small grinders, but a dedicated <a href="/best-chainsaw-sharpener/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">chainsaw sharpener</a> is safer and more accurate. A general-purpose angle grinder is not the right tool for chain teeth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Safety Gear and Habits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wear a face shield over safety glasses for any grinding, and always for wire wheels, which throw wires. Grinders throw sparks and fragments with enough force to cause serious eye injuries, and wheels can shatter. Hearing protection is essential, since grinders are loud, and leather gloves protect against sparks and hot metal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the guard between you and the wheel, use the side handle and keep both hands on the grinder. Stand to the side of the wheel's plane when starting the grinder, and run a newly fitted wheel for a minute with the guard in place before using it, because defective wheels tend to fail early. Let the grinder reach full speed before touching the work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sparks start fires. Clear away sawdust, rags, solvents and anything flammable before grinding, and watch where the sparks land. Keep a fire extinguisher nearby when grinding in a garage, and check the area for smoldering material after you finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inspect wheels before fitting them and after any drop. A cracked or chipped wheel can break apart at speed. Replace worn wheels before they get too small, store them flat and dry and never use a wheel that has been dropped or damaged. A new wheel costs much less than an emergency room visit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a cut-off wheel for side grinding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thin cut-off wheels are built to cut, not to take side loads. Grinding with the flat face of a cut-off wheel can break it apart at full speed. Use a depressed-center grinding wheel or a flap disc for grinding, and save cut-off wheels for straight cuts with the correct guard fitted.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Removing the guard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                People take the guard off to fit a bigger wheel or to reach into a tight space. Without it, nothing stands between you and a shattering wheel. Position the guard between you and the wheel, rotate it as needed and choose a smaller wheel or a different tool rather than removing it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the wheel's RPM rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every wheel lists its maximum safe speed. That rating must equal or exceed the grinder's no-load RPM. A wheel spun past its rating can burst. This is a particular risk when moving wheels between grinders or using a larger wheel on a faster tool, so check the label every time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Holding the work by hand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Holding a piece of steel in one hand while grinding with the other invites the wheel to catch and the grinder to kick. Clamp small parts in a vise and large ones to a bench. With both hands on the grinder, you can control it if something goes wrong.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting it down while the wheel spins</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A grinder without a brake keeps spinning for several seconds after you switch it off. Put it down too soon and it can walk across the bench, catch your leg or damage the work. Wait for the wheel to stop, or buy a grinder with an electronic brake.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size angle grinder should I buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy a 4-1/2-inch grinder, or one that accepts both 4-1/2-inch and 5-inch wheels. That size handles cutting bolts, rust removal, sharpening and tile, and it is light enough to control easily. Large 7-inch and 9-inch grinders are for heavy fabrication and concrete work, and most homeowners never need one, or the extra weight that comes with it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a paddle switch better than a slide switch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, yes. A no-lock paddle switch stops the grinder when you let go, so a dropped grinder shuts off. A slide switch with lock-on keeps running until switched off. Experienced users sometimes prefer slide switches for long sessions, but occasional users are safer with a paddle, ideally combined with an electronic brake.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cordless angle grinders powerful enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best brushless cordless grinders, like the Milwaukee 2880-20 and DeWalt DCG413B, are powerful enough for most work when paired with a large battery. Corded grinders still cost less and run indefinitely, so they suit long sessions at a bench. For occasional jobs around the house and yard, cordless is more convenient and powerful enough.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does a kickback brake do?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A kickback brake senses when the wheel suddenly binds, such as when a cut pinches it, and applies the electronic brake to stop the wheel quickly and shut the tool off. That limits how far the grinder twists in your hands. DeWalt says its DCG413 brake stops the wheel in two seconds or less on average.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a 5-inch wheel on a 4-1/2-inch grinder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the manufacturer says the grinder accepts 5-inch wheels and you fit the matching guard. Some grinders, like the Milwaukee 2880 and Makita XAG04Z, are rated for both. Never remove a guard to fit a larger wheel, and always check that the wheel's maximum RPM is at least the grinder's no-load speed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an angle grinder cut wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, but we do not recommend it. Woodcarving discs and chain wheels for grinders are among the most dangerous accessories sold, because they catch and kick violently. For wood, use a jigsaw, an oscillating multi-tool or a saw. Keep the grinder for metal, tile, stone and concrete, which is what it is designed for.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I sharpen mower blades with an angle grinder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Clamp the blade firmly in a vise, follow the original bevel with a flap disc or grinding wheel, and avoid overheating the edge; if the steel turns blue, you are pressing too hard. Grind both ends evenly and check the balance afterward. An unbalanced blade vibrates and can damage the mower, so remove metal carefully and evenly.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
